WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.002
-Our first guest is an
Oscar winner starring in

00:00:03.002 --> 00:00:07.540
the new film "Stillwater," which
opens in theaters July 30th.

00:00:07.540 --> 00:00:10.043
Please welcome Matt Damon!

00:00:10.043 --> 00:00:11.110
[ Cheers and applause ]
♪♪

00:00:11.110 --> 00:00:12.812
-♪ Mr. Matt Damon ♪

00:00:12.812 --> 00:00:14.980
♪ You know you can't bear ♪

00:00:14.980 --> 00:00:18.718
♪ Your mission to get your
daughter out of jail ♪

00:00:18.718 --> 00:00:20.686
♪ Mr. Matt Damon ♪

00:00:20.686 --> 00:00:22.788
♪ Got to take a chance ♪

00:00:22.788 --> 00:00:24.423
♪ If you wanna see her ♪

00:00:24.423 --> 00:00:31.064
♪ You've got to go to France ♪

00:00:31.064 --> 00:00:33.399
-Thanks, everybody.
-Oh, my God.

00:00:33.399 --> 00:00:34.867
-Look at this.
-Are you kidding me?

00:00:34.867 --> 00:00:36.536
-Oh, man.
-Come on.

00:00:36.536 --> 00:00:38.371
-That's so good.

00:00:38.371 --> 00:00:39.405
-You can't beat that
feeling, right?

00:00:39.405 --> 00:00:40.706
-No, it's the best.
That is the best.

00:00:40.706 --> 00:00:41.741
-Yeah, it is the greatest.
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:41.741 --> 00:00:43.509
Welcome back.
-Yeah.

00:00:43.509 --> 00:00:46.312
-Yeah, welcome back, everybody.
Welcome back. That's great.

00:00:46.312 --> 00:00:48.948
-Thanks for coming on the show
and coming here live in studio.

00:00:48.948 --> 00:00:50.082
We appreciate this.

00:00:50.082 --> 00:00:51.517
-And I am so lucky
to have come tonight.

00:00:51.517 --> 00:00:53.920
Like, what a big night.
-It is a big night for us, yeah.

00:00:53.920 --> 00:00:56.922
I heard this rumor, because I
know you and the family

00:00:56.922 --> 00:00:58.357
moved out to L.A. for
a few years.

00:00:58.357 --> 00:00:59.425
-Yeah.
-Probably 10, 8 years?

00:00:59.425 --> 00:01:00.960
-Yeah, eight years.

00:01:00.960 --> 00:01:02.428
-You may be coming back
to New York.

00:01:02.428 --> 00:01:03.863
Is that --
-Yeah, we're moving back.

00:01:03.863 --> 00:01:05.431
-You are coming back
to the city?

00:01:05.431 --> 00:01:06.966
-Yeah.
-I love it.

00:01:06.966 --> 00:01:09.035
-Yeah, we were supposed to
actually move back last year,

00:01:09.035 --> 00:01:11.504
but with everything
that happened,

00:01:11.504 --> 00:01:14.607
I didn't want to put
my kids in a new Zoom school.

00:01:14.607 --> 00:01:15.775
-How many Zoom schools
can we be in?

00:01:15.775 --> 00:01:17.577
-Yeah, really.
-Yeah, exactly, right?

00:01:17.577 --> 00:01:20.246
-But, you know,
we're East Coast people,

00:01:20.246 --> 00:01:21.680
and, you know,
there's no place like home,

00:01:21.680 --> 00:01:23.082
so we're coming back.

00:01:23.082 --> 00:01:24.751
-Absolutely. Oh, good.
Well, welcome back.

00:01:24.751 --> 00:01:26.819
I know you --
I have two daughters.

00:01:26.819 --> 00:01:28.621
You have four daughters?
-Yeah.

00:01:28.621 --> 00:01:32.058
-What -- We have -- I mean, it's
the cutest thing in the world.

00:01:32.058 --> 00:01:34.661
My kids are --
Winnie just turned 8 today.

00:01:34.661 --> 00:01:36.296
-Oh, wow, okay.
That's a great birthday.

00:01:36.296 --> 00:01:37.830
-Yeah.

00:01:37.830 --> 00:01:40.066
-So, I'm 10, 12, 15,

00:01:40.066 --> 00:01:42.201
and then we've got one
who just turned 23.

00:01:42.201 --> 00:01:45.971
-Wow! And can you tell
the difference, the 15 and --

00:01:45.971 --> 00:01:47.106
-Oh, yeah.

00:01:47.106 --> 00:01:48.174
-I mean, not the difference
between the two,

00:01:48.174 --> 00:01:49.508
but I mean in age.

00:01:49.508 --> 00:01:51.010
-No, I don't really know
the difference between my kids.

00:01:51.010 --> 00:01:52.111
I just...

00:01:52.111 --> 00:01:53.479
-Yeah.
I think I know you, right?

00:01:53.479 --> 00:01:55.914
-You're one of mine, right?

00:01:55.914 --> 00:01:58.051
-But, I mean, is the older
one -- is it a teenager?

00:01:58.051 --> 00:02:00.019
Is that real thing where they're
like, "Oh, dad, no.

00:02:00.019 --> 00:02:02.421
Stop."
-Oh, my, yes -- Yeah, yeah.

00:02:02.421 --> 00:02:04.723
-I'm getting
eye rolls now, and --

00:02:04.723 --> 00:02:06.058
-You are?
-Yeah, definitely.

00:02:06.058 --> 00:02:07.593
Where they used to be
my biggest fan.

00:02:07.593 --> 00:02:08.894
Anything I would do,
they would laugh at and go,

00:02:08.894 --> 00:02:10.363
"That's right, that was funny."

00:02:10.363 --> 00:02:12.932
-So, the eye rolls -- remember
when your kid started crawling

00:02:12.932 --> 00:02:14.867
and you thought it was
really cool the first day,

00:02:14.867 --> 00:02:16.736
and then like
the second day, you're like --

00:02:16.736 --> 00:02:19.405
You turn your head for
one minute and you're like,

00:02:19.405 --> 00:02:22.909
"How did you get to Cleveland?"

00:02:22.909 --> 00:02:24.710
Why are you wearing
a Guardians hat?

00:02:24.710 --> 00:02:27.780
How'd you know how to -- Right?
[ Laughter ]

00:02:27.780 --> 00:02:30.816
It's like, you know,
"Did you buy a bus ticket?"

00:02:30.816 --> 00:02:32.618
-How did it happen this fast?

00:02:32.618 --> 00:02:34.220
-So, the eye-roll thing
is like that.

00:02:34.220 --> 00:02:36.722
Like, it's cute.
"Oh, she rolled her eyes at me."

00:02:36.722 --> 00:02:38.858
-And then like within
a couple weeks, it's like --

00:02:38.858 --> 00:02:40.893
It's the first snowball
in the avalanche.

00:02:40.893 --> 00:02:43.163
It's like,
"Dad, oh, God, stop that.

00:02:43.163 --> 00:02:45.965
It's like just everything
I do is not cool.

00:02:45.965 --> 00:02:47.266
-Really?
-Yeah. Oh, absolutely.

00:02:47.266 --> 00:02:50.136
Like, if music comes on
and I start to dance,

00:02:50.136 --> 00:02:51.437
it's like
it's immediately shut down.

00:02:51.437 --> 00:02:52.972
-It's like,
"Please do not do that."

00:02:52.972 --> 00:02:55.708
-"Dude, stop it now!
You're embarrassing me."

00:02:55.708 --> 00:02:57.043
I'm like,
"We're the only ones here."

00:02:57.043 --> 00:02:59.245
-Yeah, come on.
We got to give -- We're family.

00:02:59.245 --> 00:03:00.713
-I'm not --
I'm moving to the music.

00:03:00.713 --> 00:03:02.248
It's, like,
a thing that happens.

00:03:02.248 --> 00:03:05.117
-Yeah, they won't let you do it.
But mom's cool.

00:03:05.117 --> 00:03:06.419
-Yeah, of course.

00:03:06.419 --> 00:03:07.954
I mean, they love --
Mom's the coolest thing ever,

00:03:07.954 --> 00:03:09.756
and she's got them convinced

00:03:09.756 --> 00:03:11.424
and she's kind of got me
convinced, too, so --

00:03:11.424 --> 00:03:13.492
-Yeah, exactly, yeah.
Yeah, exactly. Good for you.

00:03:13.492 --> 00:03:15.728
But I'm happy that
your family's great.

00:03:15.728 --> 00:03:18.031
I want to talk about
"The Last Duel"

00:03:18.031 --> 00:03:19.699
before we talk about
your new movie.

00:03:19.699 --> 00:03:21.901
"The Last Duel" --
you just finished this movie.

00:03:21.901 --> 00:03:24.704
You and Ben Affleck,
your buddy --

00:03:24.704 --> 00:03:26.272
I mean, you're pals forever.

00:03:26.272 --> 00:03:27.640
You co-wrote "Good Will Hunting"
together.

00:03:27.640 --> 00:03:29.108
-Yeah.

00:03:29.108 --> 00:03:30.743
-This is the first script that
you wrote together since then.

00:03:30.743 --> 00:03:31.811
-That's right.

00:03:31.811 --> 00:03:33.545
Yeah, the first movie
we've written

00:03:33.545 --> 00:03:35.381
in 25 years or something.

00:03:35.381 --> 00:03:37.584
And we wrote with someone --
We with another

00:03:37.584 --> 00:03:39.051
great writer named
Nicole Holofcener.

00:03:39.051 --> 00:03:41.754
And it's a movie -- It's going
to come out later in the year,

00:03:41.754 --> 00:03:45.157
but it's about
the last sanctioned duel

00:03:45.157 --> 00:03:47.460
in medieval France
between these two knights,

00:03:47.460 --> 00:03:50.763
one of whom accused
the other of raping his wife,

00:03:50.763 --> 00:03:52.231
and they fought to the death.

00:03:52.231 --> 00:03:55.501
And it's all based on
this history book we read.

00:03:55.501 --> 00:03:57.337
And so we adapted that.

00:03:57.337 --> 00:03:58.971
But we saw it as
a story of perspective.

00:03:58.971 --> 00:04:00.640
And so Ben and I wrote
the male perspectives,

00:04:00.640 --> 00:04:02.208
and Nicole wrote
the female perspective.

00:04:02.208 --> 00:04:03.609
And I'm really excited about it.
-Wow!

00:04:03.609 --> 00:04:05.277
-Ridley Scott directed it.
-I love it.

00:04:05.277 --> 00:04:06.278
Yeah, I love you guys
are back together.

00:04:06.278 --> 00:04:07.279
I can't wait for that.

00:04:07.279 --> 00:04:08.314
I wanted to tell you.

00:04:08.314 --> 00:04:09.815
Come back when that
comes on, too.

00:04:09.815 --> 00:04:12.585
-Yeah, we'll come back for that.
I'll bring him.

00:04:12.585 --> 00:04:15.721
-Yeah, bring him. Good.
Yeah, I want to ask him, too.

00:04:15.721 --> 00:04:18.324
But let's talk about your movie
out now, "Stillwater."

00:04:18.324 --> 00:04:20.393
Congrats on the
great reviews for this.

00:04:20.393 --> 00:04:22.661
You totally transform
in this, by the way,

00:04:22.661 --> 00:04:24.430
and I've seen everything
you're in.

00:04:24.430 --> 00:04:25.665
And you know I love you,
but I'm like,

00:04:25.665 --> 00:04:27.834
"Oh, this is like --"
You walk different.

00:04:27.834 --> 00:04:32.371
You stand different. The goatee.
The hat. The boots.

00:04:32.371 --> 00:04:34.039
I mean, you're from Oklahoma.

00:04:34.039 --> 00:04:37.943
-Yeah. We went down
there, and this guy

00:04:37.943 --> 00:04:39.111
that I play
is a roughneck, right?

00:04:39.111 --> 00:04:40.779
He works on the oil rigs.

00:04:40.779 --> 00:04:42.214
And we were really lucky.

00:04:42.214 --> 00:04:43.549
We met these great roughnecks

00:04:43.549 --> 00:04:45.084
who gave us
kind of complete access.

00:04:45.084 --> 00:04:46.886
-You went to Oklahoma? Really?
-Yeah, absolutely.

00:04:46.886 --> 00:04:48.788
And went,
and that's where all the detail

00:04:48.788 --> 00:04:50.289
and the performance
kind of came from.

00:04:50.289 --> 00:04:53.025
-Were they open arms like,
"Yeah, let me show you what's --

00:04:53.025 --> 00:04:54.660
-Yeah, I mean, they were, like,

00:04:54.660 --> 00:04:56.963
understandably wary
at first, you know?

00:04:56.963 --> 00:04:58.564
Kind of like, you know,
"What are you --

00:04:58.564 --> 00:04:59.865
What are your intentions?"

00:04:59.865 --> 00:05:01.801
When you're making a movie
about a roughneck like,

00:05:01.801 --> 00:05:02.935
"Come on, Hollywood liberal,
what are you doing?"

00:05:02.935 --> 00:05:03.969
-Yeah, yeah.

00:05:03.969 --> 00:05:05.137
"You're not making fun
of us, are you?"

00:05:05.137 --> 00:05:06.471
Yeah.
-Yeah, exactly.

00:05:06.471 --> 00:05:09.008
And once they saw that
Tom McCarthy is the director,

00:05:09.008 --> 00:05:11.377
Tom and I were,
what our movie was,

00:05:11.377 --> 00:05:13.278
and that we were just
trying to get it accurate,

00:05:13.278 --> 00:05:16.782
they were amazing and took us
around to their oil rigs,

00:05:16.782 --> 00:05:18.918
took us, you know,
to their families's, like,

00:05:18.918 --> 00:05:21.654
barbecues in the backyard,
which was just amazing.

00:05:21.654 --> 00:05:24.289
It was such
an insight into these guys

00:05:24.289 --> 00:05:26.792
and how -- They just completely
helped the performance.

00:05:26.792 --> 00:05:29.095
And also, like, you know,
we're sitting in the backyard,

00:05:29.095 --> 00:05:30.963
and, like, then --
I mean, not a backyard.

00:05:30.963 --> 00:05:32.398
Don't think back-- Like, think

00:05:32.398 --> 00:05:36.002
like big, sprawling Oklahoma,
rural, beautiful landscape.

00:05:36.002 --> 00:05:38.437
And, like, the teenagers kind of
whisper something

00:05:38.437 --> 00:05:39.672
to their dad, and their dad
is like, "Alright."

00:05:39.672 --> 00:05:40.706
And, like,
the shotguns come out.

00:05:40.706 --> 00:05:41.974
-Really?
-Yeah.

00:05:41.974 --> 00:05:44.543
Well, they wanted to
take a run at Jason Bourne.

00:05:44.543 --> 00:05:47.145
And, you know...
-Yeah, of course they do.

00:05:47.145 --> 00:05:48.414
"Jason Bourne's here."

00:05:48.414 --> 00:05:50.149
-I'm like, "Yeah, alright, kids.
Come on, let's go."

00:05:50.149 --> 00:05:51.250
"I'll show you how to..."
[ Imitates gun cocking ]

00:05:51.250 --> 00:05:52.851
-We're, like, you know,
shooting skeet

00:05:52.851 --> 00:05:53.919
and eating barbecue.

00:05:53.919 --> 00:05:55.788
It's basically
the best day ever.

00:05:55.788 --> 00:05:56.889
-Oh, my gosh.

00:05:56.889 --> 00:05:58.624
Isn't it amazing how --
I mean, gosh,

00:05:58.624 --> 00:06:00.526
you know, everything
in this country is gorgeous,

00:06:00.526 --> 00:06:01.994
but, really,
we don't even get a chance

00:06:01.994 --> 00:06:04.062
to even explore a second of it.

00:06:04.062 --> 00:06:05.698
But if you go to Oklahoma,
you're like, "Oh, my gosh.

00:06:05.698 --> 00:06:06.966
This is --"
-I know.

00:06:06.966 --> 00:06:08.734
Well, it's a great
thing about my job, actually.

00:06:08.734 --> 00:06:11.404
It's one thing that -- You know,
that research phase, like --

00:06:11.404 --> 00:06:14.673
Just because I'm an actor,
people kind of,

00:06:14.673 --> 00:06:16.108
you know, throughout my life,

00:06:16.108 --> 00:06:18.243
have accepted me into their --
You know, it's like,

00:06:18.243 --> 00:06:19.245
"Hey, I'm trying to
research this.

00:06:19.245 --> 00:06:20.245
Can you help?"

00:06:20.245 --> 00:06:21.247
And they're like,
"Yeah, come on.

00:06:21.247 --> 00:06:22.414
Let me show you how I live."

00:06:22.414 --> 00:06:24.182
-Yeah.
-And it's the coolest thing.

00:06:24.182 --> 00:06:28.153
I always leave those trips
feeling like what binds us

00:06:28.153 --> 00:06:30.156
is so much greater
than what divides us.

00:06:30.156 --> 00:06:31.323
And I always leave those trips

00:06:31.323 --> 00:06:33.259
kind of angry at politicians.

00:06:33.259 --> 00:06:35.160
-Yeah, exactly, yeah.
Exactly, right?

00:06:35.160 --> 00:06:36.796
It's like, "Oh, yeah,
we could all get along.

00:06:36.796 --> 00:06:38.798
This is -- Actually,
we're all here together."

00:06:38.798 --> 00:06:42.034
The movie is -- It's tricky.

00:06:42.034 --> 00:06:45.270
It's your daughter basically
goes to France,

00:06:45.270 --> 00:06:49.608
and she's accused of murdering
someone that she did not.

00:06:49.608 --> 00:06:51.377
A murder that
she did not commit.

00:06:51.377 --> 00:06:53.145
And you have to
go try to talk to her --

00:06:53.145 --> 00:06:54.947
she's in prison --
and figure out what

00:06:54.947 --> 00:06:56.916
actually went on
and what's going down.

00:06:56.916 --> 00:06:58.717
-Yeah. It's -- The movie starts,

00:06:58.717 --> 00:07:01.387
and you realize this roughneck,
this guy from Oklahoma,

00:07:01.387 --> 00:07:03.756
is traveling, and he ends up
in Marseille, France.

00:07:03.756 --> 00:07:04.923
And you're like,
"What is going on?"

00:07:04.923 --> 00:07:06.792
And it turns out
he's visiting his daughter,

00:07:06.792 --> 00:07:09.361
who is already four years
into a sentence for a murder

00:07:09.361 --> 00:07:10.496
that she didn't commit.

00:07:10.496 --> 00:07:13.299
And so it looks
it might be a thriller,

00:07:13.299 --> 00:07:15.735
like one of those, like,
awesome Liam Neeson movies,

00:07:15.735 --> 00:07:18.171
where he's like, "I have
a particular set of skills."

00:07:18.171 --> 00:07:20.005
You know?

00:07:20.005 --> 00:07:21.774
But it's not that at all.

00:07:21.774 --> 00:07:22.908
So I just want to --

00:07:22.908 --> 00:07:24.577
Please don't go to
the movie thinking it,

00:07:24.577 --> 00:07:26.044
because it really is
more of a drama.

00:07:26.044 --> 00:07:27.346
It's like -- You know, it's got

00:07:27.346 --> 00:07:30.149
some of those thriller elements,
but it's about a real guy

00:07:30.149 --> 00:07:31.450
trying to really do his best,

00:07:31.450 --> 00:07:33.586
and he doesn't really have
the tools that he needs.

00:07:33.586 --> 00:07:34.686
He doesn't speak the language.

00:07:34.686 --> 00:07:36.288
He doesn't understand
the culture.

00:07:36.288 --> 00:07:38.624
He's just trying to help because
he's kind of got this

00:07:38.624 --> 00:07:40.559
damaged relationship
with his daughter

00:07:40.559 --> 00:07:42.094
and he's just trying to
make it right.

00:07:42.094 --> 00:07:44.597
-But, also, as any good movie,
it has a couple good twists

00:07:44.597 --> 00:07:45.665
in the end.

00:07:45.665 --> 00:07:47.566
It's -- Whoo!
You didn't see it coming.

00:07:47.566 --> 00:07:49.001
You're like, "What?!"

00:07:49.001 --> 00:07:50.302
We were lucky
to go to a screening,

00:07:50.302 --> 00:07:52.872
which I was just psyched to go
to a movie theater.

00:07:52.872 --> 00:07:53.906
-Yeah.
-With people.

00:07:53.906 --> 00:07:55.574
-Yeah.
-And I go, "Oh, my gosh."

00:07:55.574 --> 00:07:58.844
And I was just even just
getting kind of emotional.

00:07:58.844 --> 00:08:02.548
I saw some footage
from you at Cannes.

00:08:02.548 --> 00:08:03.716
You got a standing ovation
for this film.

00:08:03.716 --> 00:08:05.050
-Yeah.

00:08:05.050 --> 00:08:06.685
-And you get a little choked up
because you go like,

00:08:06.685 --> 00:08:08.287
"Yeah, I miss all this stuff.

00:08:08.287 --> 00:08:10.022
We kind of take it for granted."

00:08:10.022 --> 00:08:11.990
-Yeah, but it's like tonight.
It's just the best.

00:08:11.990 --> 00:08:13.592
Like, we belong in
rooms together.

00:08:13.592 --> 00:08:17.363
Like, that's -- Yeah. Yeah.
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:08:17.363 --> 00:08:18.697
-I want to show everyone a clip.

00:08:18.697 --> 00:08:21.200
Here's Matt Damon in
"Stillwater."

00:08:21.200 --> 00:08:22.869
Take a look at this.

00:08:22.869 --> 00:08:24.636
-Hey!

00:08:24.636 --> 00:08:26.605
Hey! What happened?

00:08:26.605 --> 00:08:27.673
-I'm sorry.

00:08:27.673 --> 00:08:29.641
I just -- I can't talk to
that guy anymore.

00:08:29.641 --> 00:08:31.644
He was just saying
some horrible things.

00:08:31.644 --> 00:08:32.878
-Like what?

00:08:32.878 --> 00:08:35.114
-Like he just wants to put
an Arab kid in jail.

00:08:35.114 --> 00:08:37.383
You know,
he doesn't care which one.

00:08:37.383 --> 00:08:40.453
-That's it?

00:08:40.453 --> 00:08:41.887
-That's it?
What do you mean, "That's it"?

00:08:41.887 --> 00:08:43.255
He's a racist.

00:08:43.255 --> 00:08:46.292
-Okay, he's a racist.
We've still got to talk to him.

00:08:46.292 --> 00:08:48.260
-No, I don't talk to him, no.

00:08:48.260 --> 00:08:51.497
-Well, he might know something.
-No, he doesn't know something.

00:08:51.497 --> 00:08:53.999
Bill, we're not going to send
an innocent boy to jail.

00:08:53.999 --> 00:08:56.001
-My daughter is innocent.

00:08:56.001 --> 00:08:59.238
-Yeah.
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:08:59.238 --> 00:09:02.641
-So, by the way, before --
Thanks.

00:09:02.641 --> 00:09:04.877
Before you invite him out,
can I just say

00:09:04.877 --> 00:09:06.345
"Ted Lasso"
is the greatest thing,

00:09:06.345 --> 00:09:08.281
that Jason Sudeikis
is so awesome?

00:09:08.281 --> 00:09:10.783
-Yeah. I understand -- Yeah.

00:09:10.783 --> 00:09:13.118
-I was so happy for --
Yeah, It's so good.

00:09:13.118 --> 00:09:14.386
It's so good.
I'm so happy for that guy.

00:09:14.386 --> 00:09:15.954
-Yeah, me too.
I agree with you.

00:09:15.954 --> 00:09:18.257
Matt Damon, everybody.
That's what I'm talking about.

00:09:18.257 --> 00:09:20.860
"Stillwater" opens in
theaters July 30th.

